# Kotlin/Android testing

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic testing rules to Kotlin
coroutine testing, Compose UI testing, and idiomatic mocking on Android.
Applies only to `*.kt`/`*.kts` files.

## Coroutine test dispatchers

- Test a `suspend` function or a `ViewModel`'s coroutine-driven behavior
  with `kotlinx-coroutines-test`'s `runTest { }`, not a real `delay` or a
  blocking sleep — `runTest` runs on a `TestScope` whose virtual clock
  skips real delay time, so a function that internally waits still runs
  the test instantly.
- Inject a `TestDispatcher` (`StandardTestDispatcher`/
  `UnconfinedTestDispatcher`) in place of `Dispatchers.Main`/
  `Dispatchers.IO` for the code under test rather than letting it resolve
  the real platform dispatcher — set `Dispatchers.setMain(testDispatcher)`
  in a `@Before` and `Dispatchers.resetMain()` in an `@After` (or a JUnit
  rule that wraps this) so `viewModelScope`/`Dispatchers.Main` resolves to
  the test dispatcher instead of throwing or running on a background
  thread the test cannot observe.
- Prefer `StandardTestDispatcher` (queues work, you advance it explicitly
  with `advanceUntilIdle()`/`runCurrent()`) when a test needs to assert an
  intermediate state (e.g. a loading state before a result arrives); use
  `UnconfinedTestDispatcher` when the test only cares about the end state
  and wants dispatched work to run eagerly.
- Never synchronize a coroutine test with `Thread.sleep`/a fixed `delay`
  in the test body waiting for background work to finish — that is
  exactly what `runTest`'s virtual time and `advanceUntilIdle()` replace.

## Compose UI testing

- Drive Compose UI assertions through a `ComposeTestRule`
  (`createComposeRule()` for a standalone composable, or
  `createAndroidComposeRule<Activity>()` when the test needs a hosting
  Activity) — never reach into the underlying `View` tree.
- Find nodes with semantics-based finders
  (`onNodeWithText`, `onNodeWithContentDescription`,
  `onNodeWithTag(testTag)`) rather than a fragile structural/index-based
  query; add an explicit `Modifier.testTag("...")` to a composable that
  has no stable text/content-description to key off.
- Call `composeTestRule.waitUntil { ... }` (or
  `composeTestRule.awaitIdle()`/`mainClock.advanceTimeUntil { }` under
  manual clock control) to wait for an async UI update instead of a
  fixed-duration sleep in the test.
- Assert both structure and behavior: a node's presence/text via a
  semantics finder, and an interaction's effect (`performClick()` then
  assert the resulting state/text), not just that a composable rendered
  without throwing.

## Mocking at the interface boundary

- Mock a `Repository`/`ApiService`/`DataSource` interface (or another
  clearly-owned collaborator boundary) that the class under test depends
  on — never reach inside the class under test to stub its own private
  methods or fields; a test that mocks internals stops testing real
  behavior and breaks on any internal refactor.
- Prefer MockK for Kotlin code under test — it understands Kotlin
  constructs Mockito's Java-first API handles awkwardly (`final` classes
  by default, coroutines via `coEvery`/`coVerify`, extension functions,
  object mocks via `mockkObject`) — unless a project has already
  standardized on Mockito with `mockito-kotlin`; match what the project
  already uses rather than introducing a second mocking library.
- Use `coEvery { repo.fetch() } returns result` /
  `coVerify { repo.fetch() }` (MockK's coroutine-aware variants) for a
  suspend function on a mock, not the plain `every`/`verify` forms, which
  do not handle a `suspend` member correctly.
- Fake network/database responses at the repository or data-source
  interface (a fake or mocked implementation returning canned data), not
  by hitting a real network or an on-device database from a unit test —
  reserve a real backing store for an instrumented/integration test.

## Layout and lifecycle

- Unit tests for a `ViewModel`/plain Kotlin class live under
  `src/test/`; instrumented tests needing an Android context or a real
  device/emulator (Compose UI tests, `Instrumentation`-backed tests) live
  under `src/androidTest/` — match whichever the project's existing
  layout uses, do not invent a third location.
- Use JUnit rules (`MainDispatcherRule` for coroutines,
  `createComposeRule()` for Compose) to keep dispatcher/UI setup and
  teardown out of every individual test method.
